        "posts": [
          {
            "id": 591924,
            "name": "",
            "username": "Souvent22",
            "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/community.n8n.io/souvent22/{size}/191528_2.png",
            "created_at": "2026-09-27T23:47:50.695Z",
            "cooked": "<p>Hello everyone,</p>\n<p>I’m one of the creators of Ratifia, a play on “ratify.” We build<br>\nhuman-in-the-loop tooling for approvals in automated workflows that are auditable and reliable.</p>\n<p>The question I keep coming back to is whether the person receiving an approval<br>\nrequest has enough to actually decide. Not whether the workflow paused<br>\ncorrectly, but whether the human on the other end understood what they were<br>\nbeing asked to approve and what would happen when they said yes.</p>\n<p>The problem that led us here is that adding an approval step is usually<br>\nstraightforward. Making it work for the people involved is the hard part:<br>\ngetting the right context in front of the right person, handling the wait, and<br>\nbeing able to say afterward what was approved and what actually happened.</p>\n<p>Our existing design partners have shaped a lot of the product, and most of what<br>\nthey changed was on the human side of it: what the reviewer sees, how much<br>\ncontext is too much, and what they need to know about where a value came from.<br>\nNow I’d like to learn from different industries, different workflows, and<br>\ndifferent ideas about where the human belongs.</p>\n<p>I know n8n has human review for tool calls, and for plenty of cases that is<br>\nenough. Where we spend our time is the narrower question of keeping the action<br>\nthat runs tied to the approval that was given, and preserving the evidence<br>\nyou’ll need a month later.</p>\n<p>Our view is that this isn’t purely a tooling problem. It takes good workflow<br>\npatterns and software that makes those patterns practical. We want to develop<br>\nboth alongside people dealing with these problems in real projects.</p>\n<p>That’s why I’m posting instead of building another feature. I’m looking for two<br>\nor three people running production workflows with consequential or<br>\nhard-to-reverse actions: refunds, customer emails, sensitive record changes, or<br>\nmoney movement.</p>\n<p>Being a design partner means we work directly with you on the integration rather<br>\nthan pointing you at docs. We start by recording which actions would have<br>\nrequired approval, before anything blocks, so you see what it would do before<br>\nyou turn on enforcement.</p>\n<p>In return, I’m looking for a real workflow and honest feedback, including “this<br>\nis wrong.” We’ll learn the most from workflows where a mistaken action has a<br>\nreal consequence and someone is responsible for the outcome.</p>\n<p>If that sounds like your setup, I’d like to hear what you’re building and where<br>\nyour current approach falls short. Reply here or DM me. You can also take a look<br>\nat <a href=\"http://ratifia.com\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">ratifia.com</a>.</p>\n<p>P.S.<br>\nThe node lands on npm this week. Self-hosted, you install it from Settings,<br>\nCommunity nodes. On Cloud you can run the same thing with HTTP Request nodes<br>\ntoday.</p>",
